r('| Mapping | Same public port for every destination | New public port per destination |','| Mapping | Reuses a mapping for the same internal endpoint across destinations | Mapping depends on the remote IP and port |')
r('A symmetric NAT is one with destination-dependent *mapping*: talk to two servers and you appear on two different public ports. That is the hardest kind to traverse.','Those historical categories combine properties; describing mapping and filtering separately is clearer. Destination-dependent mapping can make a STUN-observed mapping unusable with another peer. It does not by itself prove that all direct connectivity checks will fail.')
r('**No inbound connections.** A host behind NAT can\'t be reached unless someone configures **port forwarding** or the app punches a hole.','**Inbound reachability needs a usable mapping and permitted traffic.** Static forwarding, mapping-control protocols, traversal or relays can provide it; a previously unused private endpoint is not automatically reachable through dynamic NAPT.')
r('NATs ship **ALGs** (application-level gateways) to rewrite these, and ALGs are a notorious source of bugs.','Some devices provide **ALGs** (application-level gateways) that rewrite protocol fields. Application-aware traversal and relays are alternatives; encryption or protocol differences can prevent an ALG from doing the required rewrite.')
r('**State in the middle.** The NAT is a single point of failure, and when it reboots or times out, every connection through it dies.','**State in the middle.** Losing an active dynamic mapping can interrupt a connection. Replicated or recoverable state and static mappings change failure behaviour; not every NAT deployment is a single unrecoverable point of failure.')
r('**Carrier-grade NAT** (CGNAT) stacks a second NAT at the ISP, in 100.64.0.0/10. Thousands of customers share one address, so IP-based rate limits and bans hit innocent neighbours, and logging must record ports as well as addresses to identify a user.','**Carrier-grade NAT** shares public IPv4 addresses among subscribers. It can add another translation layer beyond a home NAT, and RFC 6598 provides 100.64.0.0/10 for shared internal addressing. Sharing ratios vary. IP-based bans can affect unrelated subscribers; attribution needs sufficiently detailed mapping records, including protocol, ports and time. A subscriber mapping is not proof of an individual person\'s identity.')
r('NAT drops unsolicited inbound traffic as a side effect, which feels like security. But that protection comes from the *stateful filtering*, which a firewall does deliberately and IPv6 networks still do without NAT.','Translation does not define a complete security policy. Missing mappings can prevent delivery, while filtering determines which traffic may use existing mappings. IPv6 can apply stateful firewall rules without address translation.')
r('With endpoint-independent mapping, that same public address works for other peers too.','Endpoint-independent mapping can make that address reusable with other peers, but filtering, mapping lifetime and path reachability still determine whether traffic works.')
